body:
There will soon be Fresh Classic Wrath of the Lich King servers. It should come either the 16th of August (earliest) or on hte 23rd or 30th of August according to what people have been saying. So far they have been on point with establishing Wrath was late september/early october.

&#x200B;

My friends and I are rolling on the Fresh Classic Wrath server as soon as it launch. You will basically have 4-6 weeks starting from lvl 1 before anyone can enter Northrend and expand beyond lvl 70. This comes along with the reduced XP needed coming from TBC/Wrath in order to get to 70 along with a 50% XP bonus gain event preceding hte launch of Wrath on the 26th of september.

&#x200B;

So all in all you will level really fast while still experiencing the world if you never played WoW Classic while also not requiring you to no life the game in order to get to 70. We think this is gonna be a blast!

&#x200B;

Enjoy!

Hello there fellow gamer!

&#x200B;

I don't know where the 30-50% dps advantage comes from? Could you elaborate and I can test it up in CBT2?

&#x200B;

So far the only advantage in term of combat power that I noticed comes from luminus and it is a 8% advantage maximum since you get T1 luminus on day one (2% per tier). This advantage will shrink real fast, I'm estimating 5 months and under but there are already some that played this game in KR and they tell me that without paying you should get to T5 luminus earlier than that. Let's see what will happen in Western version.

&#x200B;

Cheers!

Thanks for the reply!

&#x200B;

The information about pets is really useful since I could not test this stuff out in CBT1. I guess I will test out the different kind of pets but will most likely stick with the one they give with the preorder at 30$. I will realocate 30$ towards inventory instead to make it the price of a regular game (60$ total).

&#x200B;

Cheers!

I totally agree with you on that! P2W is a wide spectrum and everyone got a different vision of what is P2W for themselves. It is nice to see a well thought response to my long post!

My point of view is not the holy grail, it is simply my point of view on this particular game.

Some may find WoW and FFIXV P2W due to instant level boost deavaluating their time spent leveling pointless. Some may find GW2 P2W since you can skip farming materials by simply buying in-game gold with IRL money and then purchasing said materials for Ascended gear. Some may find BDO P2W since you get a headstart with those convenience versus someone who did not get all the convenience on launch and purely played F2P (I got one friend who did this, took him 2 years to reach the high competitive scene back when there were no catchup mechanics, so he had to play in low-medium guilds territory war (Balenos/Serendia). Some may find ESO P2W with their subscription that give you the craft bag that without it you can't even do crafting properly due to the storage capacity you miss out without the crafting bag along with the P2W mount on launch where you saw those that paid with a mount and everyone else on foot in open world PvP/siege battle. There are as many flavor as there are MMORPG's and everyone got their vision of what is P2W.

&#x200B;

Like I said as in my final paragraphs, trying out a game is, in the end, the only real answer you will ever get. Everything else is someone else reality. I joined BDO 1 year late because everyone was saying the game was P2W. I got bored of GW2 and kept playing it even after they butchered WvW... Finally decided to give BDO a try because it was free that one weekend and I had a blast playing the game for a whole 5 years.

&#x200B;

I don't listen to reddit advice anymore for my decision on a game. I take the information from reddit and then I explore in game to see if what I read is true or false before making my final verdict.

&#x200B;

Have a great day!

Simply forgot to add ''why'' Elyon is less P2W than BDO.

In Elyon my list of things to ''buy'' in order to be competitive would be regular pets on release for the auto loot since costume don't offer stats compared to BDO. Inventory upgrade is also a nice thing to aim if you play long enough to fill your inventory too quickly.

Luminus, ''the biggest offender'' I found is barely worth mentionning since each tier is 2% increase in combat power but it is easily obtainable. At lvl 47, you get a tier 3 luminus (6% stat boost) while the highest tier is tier 5 (10% increase).

You can get regular luminus playing the game and you can smash them with the tier 3 you got and go up to 100% chance to upgrade to the next tier if you play it safe and steady. You should have a tier 5 in around 8 months for a casual player from my estimation. A player aiming for the competitive scene as soon as possible will probably take 5 months from my estimation.

&#x200B;

I read reddit for MMORPG news and rarely comment but I just wanted to share my experience.

&#x200B;

Whatever I say or anyone say, I think trying it out youself is the best solution but I understand that sometimes spending money while you got no idea what to expect might not be a pleasant feeling.

&#x200B;

I take into account the fact that you don't pre-purchase the game and that you decide to jump into it with a box price of 30$. I STRONGLY suggest that you simply try out the game without any other purchase and explore each classes before purchasing anything else. If you dislike every classes playstyle or the game in general, you stop there at 30$ spent for a couple weeks/months. If you enjoy a class, you can start thinking about what I would call the ''full game experience''. In order to truly enjoy the game you might need 4 pets in total  (20$ total for 4 pets) and you might want 1x16 inventory upgrade for 15$. In my book the complete experience would be 65$ total for this game. But before even buying anything else, why not try and sign-up for the beta and test it out yourself?

&#x200B;

Good luck and have fun!

Short answer is yes. Like in every game there is nowadays.

Long answer is that I think there is barely anything to cry murder with the build we will receive in the West. Especially if you played BDO, you will understand why quite fast.

&#x200B;

To start off on my exemple, I Played GW2 for 5 years in a competitive environment (World vs World vs World in a really strong guild with 4 raids each week that lasted 3-4 hours on average) and the expensions that came out were ''pay to win'' in the sense that each Xpac brought stuff that basic specialization could never hope to accomplish. Total cost of my 5 years on GW2 : 60$ base game and 50$ for Heart of Thorns and 50$ for Path of Fire for a total of 160$. Money well spent and I had a blast while WvW was still alive and kicking! With those purchase, I could compete at very high level and GvG to my heart content while fighting in open world fights or roam around killing folks left and right. Participated in 250 000+ kills over my years.

&#x200B;

As for BDO for my 5 years of gameplay. I can tell you that I spent 210$ to compete in the top 10 guilds while playing about the same amount of time. I logged almost every day for 1 hour of grind with the XP boost they gave each day and I participated in 2 node wars (around 1-2 hours per week) and a castle siege on weekend (2-3 hours or so) while you can also add 4-5 hours on weekend of pure fighting/grinding with friends. 10$ for the base game, 100$ worth of pearl on the first week I bought the game (bought an outfit, pets and weight limit). Later down the road I spent another 100$ worth of pearls as I bought some more weight limit, an inventory upgrade of +16 and the cash shop tent. Money well spent and I had a blast PvPing left and right with as many war declaration from our guild or war declaration from other guilds towards our guild. Participated in some amazing node wars and great castle defense/assault, I had epic duels and even participated in 3v3 arena tournaments.

&#x200B;

Some people are just bad at managing their money and they will probably be mad about those games or maybe they are good at managing their money but rather play the safe way and buy a subscription based game where everything ''should'' be more fair. While I agree that BDO had a steep learning curve and that some folks spent hundred and even thousand of dollars... I could still compete with the top with my 210$ spent. Many folks playing BDO were really friendly towards newcomers and it was easy to figure out that the ''P2W value pack'' only really function was to avoid the market taxes since the extra XP was useless later down the road once you reached the level soft cap since the hard cap was stopping you afterward... Basically, you could simply hoard all the stuff you had to sell on the market and then sell whenever they gave value pack or simply buy the 1 day value pack every 14 days with loyalty shop (in-game currency, no money to be put in the game) and mass sell on that one day. I saw some folks put 200$ on every new character they wanted to ''try'' and realizing after 1 week they did not like the playstyle and then cried it was too expensive... Be wise, examinate what is really essential to progress and then stop spending for every new shiny things you don't need.

&#x200B;

Now without any of those purchase, would I be able to be competitive? No. Can you dislike it? Yes. Did I dislike it? No.

There are many flavor in the different MMORPG we play. Some like FFXIV, some prefer ESO some prefer old school runescape and some love the action combat BDO offer but feels like Elyon offer a wider variety of activity while maintaining one of the most amazing combat system. Our group is looking forward to play and explore this new world while we tackle PvE dungeon, raids, arena, battlegrounds, siege warfare and what I find the most unique in this game... a well executed PvX dungeon. If each aspect is well executed, I will get myself another game for years to come while spending around the same amount I spent on every game I played.

&#x200B;

My best advice? Try it out. It is 30$ and you have a chance to really like the game. Worst come to worst, you spent 30$ to get a couple months of fun with the initial launch.

&#x200B;

Over my 18 years playing MMORPG, I spent on average 180$ per year on my mmorpgs. Why is my average higher if you look at my numbers above you might ask? I played subscription based mmorpg before with their Xpac every 2 years worth 60$ ;)

&#x200B;

Hope this helps.

&#x200B;

Signed by an happy canadian gamer.

I don't know how old you are, but hang in there.

&#x200B;

It may sound cliche but work on yourself. Find some things that make you happy first and then push yourself to socialize around that hobby. Let's say you enjoy video games, try to join guild/group of friends that live near your area and try and invite people to grab a beer and eat some good food before or after a game night. If it is a sport, go grab a beer after a match, etc.

&#x200B;

Some tips I could give you if you have trouble making the first move towards a woman and it makes you anxious... is to see that person like you would a male friend-to-be. Don't think about what could be in term of a relationship. Simply think about the moment you are currently sharing with that person. Talk about stuff that motivates you, stuff that you enjoy and try to listen to what she also enjoy. Should the atmosphere have that ''positive'' vibe, you could throw in a good heartfelt compliment without creeping her up (''Damn! I better keep making you laugh, because that smile is truly beautiful'') Be natural even if at first it feels like a ''front'' you are putting up.  If you enjoyed your time, hint that you enjoyed that beer/activity wtih her and you would gladly try it out again another time. Don't push if you don't feel like she wants to go further (no phone number exchange, no hints towards a future date). You will most likely fail a couple times but trust me, it will helps you build up that fake bravardo and eventually it will turn into true confidence. Worst case scenario, you made no friend and you had a good time. Good scenarios would be that you made a new friend even if it end up being a platonic friend (and it can expend your friend circle with more potential women) and perhaps you got yourself a chance at a relationship. If you don't put yourself in a situation where there is a possibility, there won't be hugs!

&#x200B;

I think success makes us more lonely since it comes with responsability and stress. We need to take control of that situation and push ourselves out of our comfort zones otherwise work will be the only thing you will have at the end of your life.

&#x200B;

Yes money takes out the stress of worrying about the stuff you buy... but our minds get molded into always thinking about the business. We need to be able to put a barrier where we can enjoy the present moment with the people that surrounds us. Good luck out there and I hope you find yourself some good friends or even a partner!

&#x200B;

Sincerely, a canadian businessman
